SOURCE
id: 1
ts: 2026-05-29T10:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Using Program Slicing in Software Maintenance
authors: Keith B. Gallagher, James R. Lyle
year: 1991
venue: IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering, vol. 17, no. 8, pp. 751-761
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/32.83912
keywords: decomposition slicing, software maintenance, independent computations, slice graph, safe extraction
cites: Weiser 1984 (program slicing)
cited_by: Bieman/Ott 1994, Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou 2011, Meyers/Binkley 2007
content:
  CORE CONCEPT: Extends Weiser's slicing criterion from <program_point, variable> pair to a
  "decomposition slice" -- a slice capturing ALL computation on a given variable, independent
  of any specific line number.
  
  DEFINITION: A decomposition slice D(P, v) for program P and variable v is the set of all
  statements that affect the value of v at ANY point in P (not just at a specific point p).
  This is equivalent to the union over all program points p of the backward slice S(P, <p, v>)
  for those p where v is defined or used.
  
  SLICE LATTICE: The set of all single-variable decomposition slices for a program P, ordered
  by set inclusion, forms a lattice. This lattice is used to define a "slice-based decomposition"
  of the program.
  
  DECOMPOSITION: Two variables v1, v2 are INDEPENDENT if their decomposition slices are
  disjoint: D(P, v1) ∩ D(P, v2) = ∅. Independent computations can be safely extracted into
  separate functions.
  
  SLICE GRAPH: The decomposition slice graph has nodes = decomposition slices for each
  output variable; edges = intersection relationships (non-empty intersection = dependence).
  Components with no edges between them represent truly independent computations.
  
  SAFE EXTRACTION CONDITION: A subset S of statements can be safely extracted into a
  separate function iff S = D(P, V') for some set of output variables V', and the remaining
  statements in P \ S depend only on the interface defined by V'.
  
  PRACTICAL USE FOR MAINTENANCE: Given a proposed change to variable v, the change's
  effects are confined to D(P, v). If D(P, v) is small relative to P, the change is low-risk.
  If D(P, v) overlaps many other decomposition slices, the change affects many computations.
  
  APPLICATION TO SPLITTING: If a function F has output variables {o1, o2, o3} where
  D(F, {o1}) and D(F, {o2, o3}) are disjoint, F can be split into F1 (computing o1) and
  F2 (computing o2, o3) with no behavioral coupling between them.
  
  RELATED EARLIER WORK: "Using program decomposition to guide modifications" (Lyle & Gallagher,
  Conf. Software Maintenance 1988). "A program decomposition scheme with applications"
  (Gallagher & Lyle, HICSS 1989).
  
  DOI: 10.1109/TSE.1991.83912
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 2
ts: 2026-05-29T10:01:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Measuring Functional Cohesion
authors: James M. Bieman, Linda M. Ott
year: 1994
venue: IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering, vol. 20, no. 8, pp. 644-657, August 1994
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.cs.colostate.edu/~bieman/Pubs/tse94new.pdf
keywords: SFC, WFC, adhesiveness, data tokens, glue tokens, superglue tokens, data slices, functional cohesion
cites: Weiser 1984, Ott/Thuss 1989/1993
cited_by: Meyers/Binkley 2007, Green et al. 2009, all subsequent slice-based cohesion work
content:
  KEY ABSTRACTION: Data slices (NOT full program slices). A DATA SLICE for output variable v
  is the sequence of data tokens in the procedure that have a dependence relationship on v.
  Specifically: a data token t is in the data slice for v iff t flows to v via def-use chains.
  
  DATA TOKEN: Any variable or constant occurrence in the procedure body.
  
  GLUE TOKEN: A data token that appears in MORE THAN ONE data slice (lies on ≥2 data slices).
  Glue tokens bind separate computation strands together.
  
  SUPERGLUE TOKEN: A data token that appears in ALL data slices (lies on every data slice).
  Superglue tokens are the core shared computation.
  
  ADHESIVENESS of token t: A(t) = (number of data slices containing t). For glue tokens,
  A(t) ≥ 2; for superglue tokens, A(t) = k (total number of data slices).
  
  WEAK FUNCTIONAL COHESION (WFC):
    WFC = |{glue tokens}| / |{all data tokens}|
    = count of tokens on ≥2 data slices / total data token count
    Range: [0, 1]. WFC=0 means no shared data between computations. WFC=1 means all tokens
    are shared across all outputs (maximally cohesive).
    INTERPRETATION: Low WFC → function computes several unrelated things → candidate for split.
  
  STRONG FUNCTIONAL COHESION (SFC):
    SFC = |{superglue tokens}| / |{all data tokens}|
    = count of tokens on ALL data slices / total data token count
    Range: [0, 1]. SFC=0 means no common computation across all outputs. SFC=1 means all
    tokens contribute to all outputs.
    INTERPRETATION: SFC=0 with WFC>0 means function has shared pairs but no universal core.
    SFC=0 means function likely has multiple independent responsibilities.
  
  ADHESIVENESS (A):
    A = (sum over all glue tokens of A(t)) / (|data tokens| × |data slices|)
    = sum_{t ∈ glue} A(t) / (|DT| × k)
    where k = number of data slices, |DT| = total data tokens.
    This is the ratio of actual adhesiveness to maximum possible adhesiveness.
  
  EXAMPLE (from thinkingintests.wordpress.com):
    Program with 3 output variables → 3 data slices.
    Total data tokens = 25. Glue token count = 12 (appear in 2 slices). Superglue = 6 (in all 3).
    WDC = 12/25 = 0.48. SDC = 6/25 = 0.24.
    DA = (12×2 + 6×3) / (25×3) = (24+18)/75 = 42/75 = 0.56.
  
  REFACTORING SIGNAL: Low WFC (few shared tokens) + low SFC (no universal core) =
  function is performing unrelated computations → extract separate functions for each
  non-overlapping data slice group.
  
  TOOL: Funco (Colorado State Univ.) -- C program analyzer implementing these metrics.
  URL: https://www.cs.colostate.edu/~bieman/funco.html
  DOI: 10.1109/32.310673
<<<END>>>

