content:
  EXTENSION OF 1994 TSE PAPER: Demonstrates that program slices provide a paradigm-independent
  abstraction for cohesion measurement. Previously cohesion was subjective; slices enable objective
  measurement.
  
  KEY CLAIM: "Program slices provide an abstraction that can be used to define important software
  attributes that can serve as a basis for measurement."
  
  SCOPE: Extends beyond procedural paradigm -- discusses applying slice-based cohesion to
  object-oriented and other paradigms.
  
  DOI: 10.1016/S0950-5849(98)00092-5
  Publisher: Elsevier Science B.V.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 4
ts: 2026-05-29T10:03:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: The Relationship Between Slices and Module Cohesion
authors: L. Ott, J. Thuss
year: 1989
venue: Proceedings of the 11th International Conference on Software Engineering (ICSE), pp. 198-204
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-Relationship-Between-Slices-And-Module-Cohesion-Ott-Thuss/dbfb779b61fd67294be00f25310cd83f1a326fc0
keywords: metric slice, module cohesion, tightness, overlap, coverage, principal variables
content:
  METRIC SLICE DEFINITION: A metric slice MS(P, v) on variable v is:
    MS(P, v) = BackwardSlice(P, <end_of_module, v>) ∪ ForwardSlice(P, d)
    for every definition d of v that appears in the backward slice.
  
  This combines backward slice from end of module with forward slices from internal
  definitions, giving a richer dependency set than pure backward slicing.
  
  OUTPUT VARIABLES: Variables modified within the module that are either (a) reference
  parameters or (b) declared outside the module's scope. These are the slicing criteria.
  
  PRINCIPAL VARIABLES: The set of output variables; metric slices are computed for each.
  
  RELATIONSHIP TO COHESION: Large overlap between metric slices → high cohesion (shared
  computation). Small/empty intersection → multiple independent tasks → low cohesion.
  
  NOTE: This 1989 paper introduced the metric slice concept; the 1993 paper at Metrics
  Symposium formalized cohesion metrics built on top.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 5
ts: 2026-05-29T10:04:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Slice Based Metrics for Estimating Cohesion
authors: L. Ott, J. Thuss
year: 1993
venue: Proceedings of the 1st International Software Metrics Symposium, pp. 71-81
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/The-Relationship-Between-Slices-And-Module-Cohesion-Ott-Thuss/dbfb779b61fd67294be00f25310cd83f1a326fc0
keywords: tightness, min-coverage, max-coverage, overlap, parallelism, cohesion metrics
content:
  METRICS DEFINED (built on metric slices for principal variables {v1,...,vk}, module M):
  
  Let Si = metric_slice(M, vi), |Si| = size of slice i (in statements), |M| = module size.
  Let S_all = S1 ∩ S2 ∩ ... ∩ Sk (intersection of ALL slices).
  
  TIGHTNESS: T(M) = |S_all| / |M|
    = size of intersection of all slices / total module size
    Range: [0, 1]. T=1 means entire module is shared by all outputs (maximally tight).
    T=0 means no statement contributes to all outputs simultaneously.
    High tightness → single-purpose function. Low tightness → multiple unrelated tasks.
  
  MIN-COVERAGE: MinCov(M) = min_i(|Si|) / |M|
    = size of smallest slice / total module size
    Characterizes the "thinnest" output computation.
  
  MAX-COVERAGE: MaxCov(M) = max_i(|Si|) / |M|
    = size of largest slice / total module size
  
  COVERAGE: Cov(M) = (1/k) * sum_i(|Si|) / |M|
    = average slice size / total module size (Weiser's metric, adopted here)
    Range: [0, 1]. Coverage close to 1 means most of module participates in each output.
  
  OVERLAP: OL(M) = average over all pairs (i,j) of |Si ∩ Sj| / |Si ∪ Sj|
    Or Weiser's definition: (1/k) * sum_i( |Si ∩ S_all| / |Si| )
    Measures average pairwise sharing relative to union.
  
  PARALLELISM: PAR(M) = 1 - (1/k) * sum_i(|Si ∩ S_all| / |Si|)
    = 1 - Tightness-normalized version
    High parallelism → slices are mostly non-overlapping → independent computations.
    
  NOTE: Low Tightness + high Parallelism = strong signal to split function.
  NOTE: Sources vary slightly on exact Overlap formula; see Green et al. 2009 for survey.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 6
ts: 2026-05-29T10:05:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Program Slicing (Weiser 1984) — metrics section
authors: Mark D. Weiser
year: 1984
venue: IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering, vol. SE-10, no. 4, pp. 352-357, July 1984
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/TSE.1984.5010248
keywords: program slicing, tightness, overlap, coverage, cohesion, slicing criterion
content:
  SLICING CRITERION: A pair <p, V> where p is a program point and V is a set of variables.
  A slice S(P, <p,V>) is the set of all statements of P that might affect the values of V at p.
  
  COHESION METRICS (from Weiser's original paper, section on metrics):
  
  COVERAGE: ratio of average slice size to module length.
    Cov(M) = (1/k) * sum_{i=1}^{k} |S_i| / |M|
    where k = number of slices (one per output variable), |S_i| = size of slice i, |M| = module size.
    High coverage → most statements in module contribute to most outputs → cohesive.
  
  TIGHTNESS: ratio of size of intersection of all slices to module length.
    T(M) = |S_1 ∩ S_2 ∩ ... ∩ S_k| / |M|
    High tightness → a large "core" shared by all outputs → single-minded module.
    T=0 means no statement is shared by all outputs → no universal computation core.
  
  OVERLAP: average fraction of each slice that is shared with all other slices.
    OL(M) = (1/k) * sum_{i=1}^{k} |S_i ∩ S_all| / |S_i|
    where S_all = intersection of all slices.
    High overlap → each slice shares most of its content with the universal core.
  
  KEY INSIGHT from Longworth (cited in subsequent literature): Coverage, a modified Overlap,
  and Tightness can differentiate high from low cohesion. Tightness favors "single thought"
  modules. Coverage tolerates large slices without requiring them to overlap.
  
  NOTE: Weiser proposed these metrics but did not identify specific software quality attributes
  they measure. Ott/Thuss (1989, 1993) and Bieman/Ott (1994) grounded them in cohesion theory.
  
  DOI: 10.1109/TSE.1984.5010248
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 7
ts: 2026-05-29T10:06:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Precise Interprocedural Chopping
authors: Thomas Reps, Genevieve Rosay
year: 1995
venue: Proceedings of the 3rd ACM SIGSOFT Symposium on Foundations of Software Engineering (FSE-3), pp. 41-49
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://research.cs.wisc.edu/wpis/papers/fse95b.pdf
keywords: program chopping, chop, source, target, interprocedural, context-sensitive, PDG, SDG
cites: Jackson/Rollins 1994 (first chopping paper), Horwitz/Reps/Binkley 1990 (SDG)
cited_by: Krinke 2004 (barrier chopping), Giffhorn 2011 (advanced chopping)
content:
  CHOPPING DEFINITION: A chop C(s, t) from source criterion s to target criterion t
  consists of all nodes n in the SDG such that there exists an interprocedurally realizable
  path from s to n AND from n to t. More precisely:
    chop(s, t) = { v | ∃ realizable path s →* v →* t in SDG }
  
  INTUITION: "What program elements v serve to transmit effects from source s to target t?"
  Equivalently: "What flows from s to t?" or "What computation mediates s's effect on t?"
  
  RELATION TO SLICING:
    backward_slice(t) = chop(entire_program, t)
    forward_slice(s) = chop(s, entire_program)
    chop(s, t) ⊆ backward_slice(t) ∩ forward_slice(s)
    [Chop is STRICTLY SMALLER than the intersection -- more precise!]
  
  CONTEXT-SENSITIVITY: Reps/Rosay were FIRST to give context-sensitive interprocedural
  chopping, distinguishing different call sites' contexts. Jackson/Rollins 1994 had defined
  chopping but only intraprocedurally (same procedure restriction).
  
  ALGORITHM: Two-phase algorithm on SDG:
    Phase 1: Forward reachability from s (context-sensitive, respecting call/return edges)
    Phase 2: Backward reachability from t (context-sensitive), intersected with Phase 1 result.
  COMPLEXITY: O(E² × N) where E = SDG edges, N = SDG nodes.
  
  REFACTORING USE: chop(s, t) answers "what part of this function is about computing t from s?"
  For pflow: given a data source (e.g., an input parameter read) and a sink (e.g., return or
  output variable), the chop identifies exactly which statements belong to that computation.
  Statements NOT in any meaningful chop are dead code or uncoupled utility code.
  
  MULTI-CHOP for function splitting: Compute chop(inputs, o1) and chop(inputs, o2). If
  these are disjoint, the function can be cleanly split at the boundary between them.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 8
ts: 2026-05-29T10:07:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Chopping: A Generalization of Slicing
authors: Daniel Jackson, Eugene J. Rollins
year: 1994
venue: CMU-CS-94-169, Technical Report, Carnegie Mellon University, School of Computer Science, July 1994
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/ADA282683.pdf
keywords: program chopping, generalization of slicing, source set, sink set, PDG, forward slice, backward slice
content:
  ORIGINAL CHOPPING PAPER (intraprocedural version).
  
  DEFINITION: Given source set SRC and sink set SNK (sets of variable instances at program points),
  a chop is the graph of statements transmitting effects from SRC to SNK.
    chop(SRC, SNK) = forward_slice(SRC) ∩ backward_slice(SNK)
  
  GENERALIZATION: Chopping subsumes slicing as special cases:
    backward_slice(snk) = chop(∅ with all program inputs as source, snk)
    forward_slice(src) = chop(src, ∅ with all outputs as sink)
  
  LIMITATION: Jackson/Rollins restricted source and target to same procedure. Reps/Rosay 1995
  extended to full interprocedural chopping.
  
  ALTERNATIVE CHOP DEFINITION from Reps/Rosay: chop(s,t) is NOT simply intersection of forward
  and backward slices when context-sensitivity matters. The intersection gives an overapproximation;
  context-sensitive chopping is more precise.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 9
ts: 2026-05-29T10:08:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Restructuring Programs by Tucking Statements into Functions
authors: Arun Lakhotia, Jean-Christophe Deprez
year: 1998
venue: Information and Software Technology, vol. 40, no. 11-12, pp. 677-689
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0950584998000913
keywords: Tuck, Wedge, Split, Fold, extract function, refactoring, slice-based, SESE region
cites: Gallagher/Lyle 1991, Weiser 1984
cited_by: Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou 2009/2011, Maruyama 2001, SBSRE 2023
content:
  THE TUCK TRANSFORMATION: A three-step program transformation to extract a function from
  a larger one. Consists of Wedge + Split + Fold.
  
  STEP 1 - WEDGE:
    Given a set of "seed statements" (statements the developer wants to extract), the Wedge
    operation finds the smallest single-entry, single-exit (SESE) region that contains all
    seed statements AND all statements that the seed statements depend on (the "slice" of
    the seed statements within the function). This SESE region is the unit of extraction.
    
    Formally: Wedge(F, seed) = the slice S ⊆ F such that:
      - seed ⊆ S
      - S is a SESE region (single control-flow entry, single control-flow exit)
      - S is minimal (smallest SESE containing seed's full dependency closure)
    
    The SESE constraint ensures extractability -- arbitrary subsets of statements cannot
    be extracted because they may not form valid control flow.
  
  STEP 2 - SPLIT:
    Partition the statements in the Wedge into "meaningful function" -- separate computations.
    This is driven by decomposition slicing: compute decomposition slices of outputs within
    the Wedge and identify independent sub-computations.
  
  STEP 3 - FOLD:
    The split sub-computation is extracted into a new function. A call to the new function
    replaces the original statements in F. The interface (parameters, return values) is
    determined by the data flow crossing the extraction boundary.
  
  SAFETY: Safe if the extracted statements form a closed SESE region and the interface
  correctly captures all data flowing in/out of the extracted region.
  
  KEY INSIGHT: The Wedge step uses program slicing to grow the seed into a minimal complete
  computation. The Split step uses decomposition slicing to find sub-computations within the
  Wedge. Fold replaces the statements with a call.
  
  DOI: 10.1016/S0950-5849(98)00091-3
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 10
ts: 2026-05-29T10:10:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: An Empirical Study of Slice-Based Cohesion and Coupling Metrics
authors: T. Meyers, D. W. Binkley
year: 2007
venue: ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM), vol. 17, no. 1, pp. 1:1-1:36, December 2007
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.cs.loyola.edu/~binkley/papers/tosem07-slice-metrics.pdf
keywords: cohesion metrics, coupling metrics, SFC, WFC, inflow, outflow, empirical study, slice-based
cites: Bieman/Ott 1994, Ott/Thuss 1989/1993, Weiser 1984, Harman et al. 1997
content:
  SCOPE: First large-scale empirical study comparing all major slice-based cohesion AND coupling
  metrics across real C programs.
  
  COHESION METRICS STUDIED:
    - Tightness (T): |S_all| / |M|  (Weiser 1984)
    - Coverage (Cov): avg(|S_i|) / |M|  (Weiser 1984)
    - Overlap (OL): avg over pairs of Jaccard similarity  (Weiser 1984)
    - WFC: glue_tokens / total_data_tokens  (Bieman/Ott 1994)
    - SFC: superglue_tokens / total_data_tokens  (Bieman/Ott 1994)
    - Adhesiveness (A): sum(A(t)) / (|DT| × k)  (Bieman/Ott 1994)
    - MinCoverage, MaxCoverage  (Ott/Thuss 1993)
  
  COUPLING METRICS STUDIED (based on Harman et al. 1997 framework):
    INFLOW into function f: The set of definitions OUTSIDE f that appear in the backward slice
    taken with respect to the principal variables of f. Captures how much f depends on external data.
    
    OUTFLOW from function f: The set of components OUTSIDE f that have f's principal variables
    in their backward slice. Captures how much external code depends on f's outputs.
    
    COUPLING(f) = weighted average of f's coupling to all other functions.
    Meyers/Binkley extended Harman et al.'s 1997 two-example paper to a full empirical study.
  
  EMPIRICAL FINDINGS:
    - Metrics can identify deteriorating software quality as programs age (change over versions).
    - Inter-metric correlations are high within cohesion group but coupling metrics provide
      distinct viewpoint from cohesion metrics.
    - SFC and Tightness provide similar signals for single-responsibility assessment.
    - No empirical work on slice-based coupling had been done before this paper since Harman 1997.
    - Baseline measures established: typical C function has Tightness ~0.2-0.4, Coverage ~0.5-0.7.
  
  DOI: 10.1145/1314493.1314495
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 11
ts: 2026-05-29T10:11:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Slice-Based Measurement of Coupling
authors: Mark Harman, M. Okunlawon, Y. Sivagurunathan, S. Danicic
year: 1997
venue: Proceedings of the IEEE/ACM ICSE Workshop on Process Modelling and Empirical Studies of Software Evolution, Boston, MA, pp. 28-32
quality: WORKSHOP
url: https://kclpure.kcl.ac.uk/portal/en/publications/slice-based-measurement-of-coupling/
keywords: slice-based coupling, inflow, outflow, principal variables, procedure coupling
content:
  FIRST PAPER applying program slicing to coupling measurement.
  Position paper with two small examples; no empirical validation.
  
  FRAMEWORK:
    Given function f with principal variables {v1,...,vk} (output variables):
    
    INFLOW(f) = definitions of variables OUTSIDE f that appear in
      backward_slice(f, <end_of_f, vi>) for any vi.
    Captures: "what external data does f depend on beyond its direct parameters?"
    
    OUTFLOW(f) = functions/components g OUTSIDE f such that
      any principal variable vi of f appears in backward_slice(g, <end_of_g, vj>)
      for some output variable vj of g.
    Captures: "what external code depends on f's outputs (beyond direct return value)?"
    
    COUPLING(f) = some combination of inflow + outflow sizes (weighted).
    
  EXTENDED by: Meyers/Binkley TOSEM 2007 (defined coupling(f) as weighted average across
  all other functions), who provided the first empirical study.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 12
ts: 2026-05-29T10:12:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Dependence Clusters in Source Code
authors: Mark Harman, David Binkley, Keith Gallagher, Nicolas Gold, Jens Krinke
year: 2009
venue: ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems (TOPLAS), vol. 32, no. 1, November 2009
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/citation.cfm?id=1596528
keywords: dependence cluster, monotone slice-size graph, MSG, complexity, refactoring difficulty, mutually inter-dependent
cites: Binkley/Harman 2005 (locating clusters), earlier Harman et al. work on slicing
content:
  DEFINITION: A dependence cluster is a set of program statements S such that every statement
  in S is mutually inter-dependent with every other statement in S. Formally:
    For all s1, s2 ∈ S: s1 ∈ backward_slice(s2) AND s2 ∈ backward_slice(s1).
  
  Equivalently: S is a dependence cluster iff all pairwise backward slices within S are identical
  (when restricted to S). Every statement depends on every other statement.
  
  MONOTONE SLICE-SIZE GRAPH (MSG): A visualization tool for dependence clusters.
    X-axis: each program statement (ordered by increasing backward slice size)
    Y-axis: backward slice size as % of total program
    CLUSTER SIGNATURE: A dependence cluster appears as a "plateau" -- many consecutive statements
    with identical slice size (the whole cluster has the same slice, since all are inter-dependent).
    Preceded by a "cliff face" (sudden jump in slice size when entering the cluster).
  
  B-MSG: MSG based on backward slice sizes.
  F-MSG: MSG based on forward slice sizes.
  
  ALGORITHM FOR FINDING CLUSTERS:
    Naive: compute all pairwise backward slices (O(n²) slices).
    Efficient approximation: compare slice SIZES (same-size heuristic).
    Approximation precision: ≥97.76% across studied programs.
  
  EMPIRICAL RESULTS (large-scale C program study):
    - Dependence clusters are "ubiquitous" in real programs.
    - Many programs have clusters containing 30-70% of their statements.
    - Global variables are the primary cause of large dependence clusters.
    - Programs with large clusters are structurally difficult to refactor/test/maintain.
  
  REFACTORING IMPLICATION: A function containing a large dependence cluster CANNOT be
  easily split -- all statements in the cluster are mutually entangled. To split, the
  cluster root cause (usually global variables/shared state) must first be eliminated.
  Identifying cluster boundaries tells developers WHERE refactoring is blocked.
  
  FOR pflow: Compute backward slices for each statement in function. Find statements with
  identical slice sets (or same-size approximation). These form the irreducible core.
  Statements outside the core are candidates for extraction.
  
  DOI: 10.1145/1596524.1596528
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 13
ts: 2026-05-29T10:13:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Locating Dependence Clusters and Dependence Pollution
authors: David Binkley, Mark Harman
year: 2005
venue: Proceedings of the 21st IEEE International Conference on Software Maintenance (ICSM 2005)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/4175874_Locating_dependence_clusters_and_dependence_pollution
keywords: dependence cluster, dependence pollution, global variables, MSG, locating clusters
content:
  PRECURSOR to the TOPLAS 2009 paper (Harman et al. 2009).
  
  DEPENDENCE POLLUTION: The phenomenon where global variables cause many unrelated
  program elements to be included in each other's slices, creating artificially large
  dependence clusters. A polluting variable v causes large clusters because many
  functions both read and write v, creating spurious mutual dependencies.
  
  LOCATING CLUSTERS: Algorithm to find dependence clusters using the MSG.
  Key observation: In an MSG, a plateau (flat section) = a dependence cluster.
  The plateau height gives the cluster size. The cliff face before the plateau marks
  the entry boundary into the cluster.
  
  RELATED: Identifying "linchpin vertices" -- the specific variable/statement whose
  removal would break up a large cluster. Linchpins are the primary refactoring targets.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 14
ts: 2026-05-29T10:14:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Coherent Dependence Clusters
authors: Syed Islam, Jens Krinke, David Binkley, Mark Harman
year: 2010
venue: Proceedings of the 9th ACM SIGPLAN-SIGSOFT Workshop on Program Analysis for Software Tools and Engineering (PASTE 2010)
quality: WORKSHOP
url: http://www0.cs.ucl.ac.uk/staff/M.Harman/paste10.pdf
keywords: coherent cluster, same-slice cluster, SCG, slice-cluster graph, backward and forward slices
content:
  COHERENT DEPENDENCE CLUSTER DEFINITION:
  A coherent dependence cluster is a set S of statements where ALL statements in S
  have BOTH the same backward slice AND the same forward slice.
  
  Formally: S is coherent iff ∀ s1, s2 ∈ S:
    backward_slice(s1) = backward_slice(s2)  AND
    forward_slice(s1) = forward_slice(s2)
  
  DIFFERENCE from basic dependence cluster: Basic clusters require mutual backward dependency.
  Coherent clusters additionally require identical forward effects (same impact on the rest).
  Coherent clusters are STRONGER (more tightly constrained) than basic dependence clusters.
  
  SLICE/CLUSTER-SIZE GRAPH (SCG): A visualization showing three landscapes:
    1. Increasing slice sizes (solid black line)
    2. Corresponding same-slice cluster sizes (light gray line)  
    3. Corresponding coherent-slice cluster sizes (dashed red line)
  
  EMPIRICAL RESULTS (30 programs):
    - 23 of 30 programs have coherent clusters containing ≥10% of program statements.
    - Despite tighter constraints, coherent clusters are abundant in practice.
    - Approximation (same-size-slice instead of same-slice) has minimum precision of 97.76%.
  
  IMPLICATION FOR REFACTORING: Coherent clusters identify code that is so tightly coupled
  it has identical data-flow context from multiple different perspectives. These are the
  hardest regions to decompose -- they represent a "single atomic computation" even if
  superficially complex.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 15
ts: 2026-05-29T10:20:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Identification of Extract Method Refactoring Opportunities for the Decomposition of Methods
authors: Nikolaos Tsantalis, Alexander Chatzigeorgiou
year: 2011
venue: Journal of Systems and Software, vol. 84, no. 10, pp. 1757-1782, October 2011
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0164121211001191
keywords: complete computation slice, object state slice, PDG, extract method, behavior preservation, cohesion
cites: Maruyama 2001, Lakhotia/Deprez 1998, Gallagher/Lyle 1991
cited_by: SBSRE 2023, multiple subsequent automated refactoring papers
content:
  CORE CONTRIBUTION: Algorithm for AUTOMATICALLY identifying all possible Extract Method
  refactoring opportunities in a Java method using PDG-based slicing.
  
  COMPLETE COMPUTATION SLICE (Definition 3.3.1 in paper):
    A slice CS(M, v) for variable v is a "complete computation slice" iff it contains:
    - All statements that contribute to the FINAL value of v (backward slice from last use/def)
    - AND the slice forms a self-contained computational unit that can be extracted
    
    Implemented via full PDG backward reachability from the last def/use of v within method M.
  
  OBJECT STATE SLICE:
    Additional slice type capturing statements that modify the state of a given object o.
    Includes all method calls on o and assignments to o's fields within M.
    Needed for object-oriented programs where state mutation creates implicit dependencies.
  
  PDG CONSTRUCTION (Section 3.1):
    - Nodes: assignment statements and control predicates
    - Edges: flow dependence (def→use) + control dependence (predicate→governed stmt)
    - Object state treated as additional implicit output
  
  BEHAVIOR PRESERVATION RULES (Section 3.4) -- extraction is UNSAFE if:
    1. Duplication of statements affecting object state would be required
    2. Duplication of class instance creation statements required
    3. Existing anti-dependences (write-after-read) would be violated
    4. Existing output-dependences (write-after-write) would be violated
    5. Extracted method would have no useful functionality (trivial)
  
  BLOCK-BASED SLICING: Extension where slices are restricted to "block-based regions" --
  consecutive basic-block sequences. Prevents non-contiguous extractions that would be
  confusing to developers.
  
  EARLIER RELATED PAPER: Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou, "Identification of Extract Method
  Refactoring Opportunities," CSMR 2009, Kaiserslautern. This 2009 paper introduced the
  approach; JSS 2011 extended it with additional behavior preservation rules.
  
  TOOL: JDeodorant (Eclipse plugin for Java, MIT license)
  GitHub: https://github.com/tsantalis/JDeodorant
  Last activity: v5.0.85, January 5, 2025.
  
  EMPIRICAL RESULTS: Tested on open-source Java projects. Compared against independent human
  evaluators' Extract Method recommendations. Precision and recall measured.
  
  COHESION IMPACT: Evaluated using slice-based cohesion metrics (tightness, coverage);
  extracted methods show improved cohesion vs. original method.
  
  DOI: 10.1016/j.jss.2011.05.016
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 16
ts: 2026-05-29T10:21:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Supporting Single Responsibility Through Automated Extract Method Refactoring (SBSRE)
authors: Alireza Ardalani, Mohsen Rezaei Shirsavar, Mohammad Reza Keyvanpour, Mohamad Ali Nematbakhsh
year: 2023
venue: Empirical Software Engineering (Springer), published 2023; also arXiv:2305.03428
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.03428
keywords: output-based slicing, single responsibility principle, SBSRE, extract method, SRP violations
cites: Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou 2011, Maruyama 2001, JDeodorant
content:
  KEY INNOVATION: Output-based slicing (vs. complete computation slice of Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou).
  
  OUTPUT-BASED SLICE DEFINITION:
    An output-based slice OBS(M, o) focuses on variables used in OUTPUT INSTRUCTIONS -- i.e.,
    variables that appear in return statements, assignments to global variables, or reference
    parameter modifications. This captures "what responsibility does M discharge via output o?"
    
    DIFFERENCE from Tsantalis: Complete computation slice uses final def/use of any local variable.
    Output-based slice focuses specifically on outputs exposed to callers, aligning with SRP.
  
  ALGORITHM:
    1. Identify output instructions O = {return, global writes, ref-param writes} in method M
    2. For each output instruction oi ∈ O: compute OBS(M, oi) via backward PDG slicing
    3. If OBS(M, oi) ∩ OBS(M, oj) = ∅ for i≠j: two output instructions have no shared data flow
       → they implement INDEPENDENT responsibilities → Extract Method opportunity
    4. Apply behavior preservation rules before proposing extraction
  
  BEHAVIOR PRESERVATION: Inherited from Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou plus additional rules specific
  to output-based extraction.
  
  EMPIRICAL RESULTS:
    - 29.6% improvement in precision vs. state-of-the-art (JDeodorant-based)
    - 12.1% improvement in recall
    - 20% average improvement in method-level cohesion metrics after refactoring
  
  TOOL: SBSRE -- Eclipse plugin for Java
  GitHub: https://github.com/Alireza-Ardalani/SBSRE (MIT license, Java 100%)
  
  SLICE-BASED COHESION METRICS USED FOR EVALUATION:
    WFC and SFC (Bieman/Ott 1994) measured before and after refactoring.
  
  DOI: 10.1007/s10664-023-10427-3
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 17
ts: 2026-05-29T10:22:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Automated Method-Extraction Refactoring by Using Block-Based Slicing
authors: Katsuhisa Maruyama
year: 2001
venue: Proceedings of the 2001 Symposium on Software Reusability, pp. 31-40
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/221563951_Automated_method-extraction_refactoring_by_using_block-based_slicing
keywords: block-based slicing, extract method, backward slicing, basic block, method extraction, Java
cites: Lakhotia/Deprez 1998, Gallagher/Lyle 1991
content:
  FIRST automated extract-method refactoring system using slicing (for Java).
  
  APPROACH:
    1. Developer specifies TARGET VARIABLE of interest
    2. Tool computes STATIC BACKWARD SLICE of target variable within method
    3. Slice is restricted to a BLOCK-BASED REGION (consecutive basic blocks)
    4. The region is extracted as a new method
  
  BLOCK-BASED REGION: A maximal sequence of consecutive basic blocks (contiguous control flow)
  that contains the backward slice. Prevents non-contiguous extractions.
  
  KEY LIMITATION (noted in Tsantalis 2011): Requires developer to specify target variable.
  Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou 2011 generalized this to automatic enumeration of ALL possible
  complete computation slices.
  
  CONTRIBUTION: Simplified Larsen/Harrold 1996 interprocedural slicing algorithm to intraprocedural,
  then introduced block-based regions for practical method extraction.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 18
ts: 2026-05-29T10:23:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Restructuring Functions with Low Cohesion
authors: N. Gold, M. Harman (and possibly others)
year: 1999
venue: Proceedings of the 6th Working Conference on Reverse Engineering (WCRE 1999), IEEE
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/806945
keywords: function restructuring, pairwise cohesion, output variable partitioning, slice-based, object-oriented reengineering
cites: Bieman/Ott 1994, Ott/Thuss 1993, Gallagher/Lyle 1991
content:
  CORE ALGORITHM for splitting a low-cohesion function F:
  
  STEP 1 - COMPUTE PAIRWISE COHESION:
    For each pair of output variables (oi, oj) of F, compute their pairwise cohesion.
    Pairwise cohesion C(oi, oj) measures how much of the slice for oi overlaps with the
    slice for oj:
    C(oi, oj) = |S(oi) ∩ S(oj)| / |S(oi) ∪ S(oj)|  (Jaccard similarity of slices)
    
  STEP 2 - PARTITION OUTPUT VARIABLES:
    Use the pairwise cohesion values to cluster/partition the output variables into groups
    {G1, G2, ..., Gm} where variables within a group have high cohesion and variables
    across groups have low cohesion.
    
  STEP 3 - EXTRACT FUNCTIONS:
    For each group Gk, use program slicing to identify the statements performing computations
    for Gk: S(Gk) = ∪_{oi ∈ Gk} S(F, oi).
    Create a new function Fk containing S(Gk).
    
  RESULT: F is replaced by calls to F1, F2, ..., Fm.
  Each Fi has higher cohesion than original F.
  
  MOTIVATION: Re-architecting legacy procedural systems into OO architecture.
  High-cohesion functions → better candidates for becoming methods of specific classes.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 19
ts: 2026-05-29T10:24:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Interprocedural Slicing Using Dependence Graphs
authors: Susan Horwitz, Thomas Reps, David Binkley
year: 1990
venue: ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems (TOPLAS), vol. 12, no. 1, pp. 26-60, January 1990
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.csa.iisc.ac.in/~raghavan/CleanedPav2011/horwitz-sdg-slicing-1990.pdf
keywords: SDG, system dependence graph, interprocedural slicing, PDG, summary edges, calling context
cites: Weiser 1984, Ferrante/Ottenstein/Warren 1987
cited_by: All subsequent interprocedural slicing work; Reps/Rosay 1995 (chopping)
content:
  PROGRAM DEPENDENCE GRAPH (PDG) for single-procedure programs:
    Vertices: entry vertex + assignment statements + control predicates + initial defs + final uses
    Edges:
      - CONTROL DEPENDENCE EDGE: v1 →c v2 (labeled true/false)
        v1 is entry or predicate; v2 will execute whenever v1's condition matches edge label.
      - FLOW DEPENDENCE EDGE: v1 →f v2
        v1 defines variable x, v2 uses x, no intervening redefinition on some path.
        Subtypes: loop-carried (v1 →lc(L) v2) vs loop-independent (v1 →li v2)
      - DEF-ORDER DEPENDENCE EDGE: v1 →d v2
        Both v1 and v2 define x, v1 precedes v2 in some execution but there's no use of x
        between them on that path (matters for determining which definition reaches uses).
    
    INTRAPROCEDURAL SLICE: Reachability in PDG backward from target vertex.
    Linear time algorithm (O(V+E)).
  
  SYSTEM DEPENDENCE GRAPH (SDG) for interprocedural programs:
    = collection of PDGs (one per procedure) connected by:
    - CALL EDGES: call_vertex →call callee_entry
    - PARAMETER-IN EDGES: actual_in →pi formal_in (at call sites)
    - PARAMETER-OUT EDGES: formal_out →po actual_out (at return)
    - SUMMARY EDGES: actual_in →sum actual_out
      A summary edge represents the TRANSITIVE effect of a procedure call: if formal_in can
      affect formal_out, then actual_in transitively affects actual_out via the call.
      Summary edges are precomputed to enable context-sensitive slicing.
  
  TWO-PASS ALGORITHM for interprocedural slicing:
    Pass 1 (ascending): Start from target, traverse backward. When reaching a FORMAL_OUT node,
      cross into caller's actual_out via PARAMETER-OUT edge. Then traverse CALL edges and
      PARAMETER-IN edges (going UP call graph). Also follow SUMMARY edges.
      Do NOT cross call edges from callee to caller via call edges in this pass.
    Pass 2 (descending): From criteria accumulated in Pass 1, traverse within each procedure
      using only intra-procedural edges (flow, control, def-order). Cross CALL edges going DOWN.
    
    CORRECTNESS: Two-pass correctly handles calling context by not mixing contexts from
    different call sites (Weiser's algorithm failed here).
    
  COMPLEXITY: O(E³) construction of summary edges; O(E) slicing given summary edges.
  
  COMMERCIAL TOOL: CodeSurfer (GrammaTech) implements this algorithm.
  PATENT: SDGs covered by U.S. patent (Wisconsin Alumni Research Foundation).
  
  Earlier version: PLDI 1988 proceedings. Journal version in TOPLAS 1990.
  DOI: 10.1145/140731.140733
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 20
ts: 2026-05-29T10:25:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: The Program Dependence Graph and Its Use in Optimization
authors: Jeanne Ferrante, Karl J. Ottenstein, Joe D. Warren
year: 1987
venue: ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems (TOPLAS), vol. 9, no. 3, pp. 319-349, July 1987
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/24039.24041
keywords: PDG, program dependence graph, control dependence, data dependence, optimization
cited_by: Horwitz/Reps/Binkley 1990, all PDG-based slicing work
content:
  FOUNDATIONAL PDG DEFINITION:
  
  CONTROL DEPENDENCE: Statement Y is control-dependent on statement X iff:
    1. X has at least two successors in the CFG
    2. There exists a path from X to Y in the CFG on which X's next control decision leads
       to eventual execution of Y
    3. BUT there also exists a path from X that bypasses Y (Y is not guaranteed to execute)
    
    Formally using post-dominance: Y is control-dependent on X iff:
    - Y post-dominates some successor of X in CFG
    - Y does NOT post-dominate X itself
    
  DATA DEPENDENCE EDGE (flow/anti/output):
    - Flow: def v → use v (a value is computed and used)
    - Anti: use v → def v (a value is used before being redefined)
    - Output: def v → def v (two definitions of same variable)
  
  PDG CONSTRUCTION: Build CFG → compute dominators/post-dominators → derive control deps →
  compute reaching defs → add data dep edges.
  
  SLICING ON PDG (Ottenstein/Ottenstein 1984 observation): 
    backward_slice(v) = backward reachability from v in PDG.
    Linear time: O(V+E) per slice.
  
  DOI: 10.1145/24039.24041
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 21
ts: 2026-05-29T10:26:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Fine Slicing: Theory and Applications for Computation Extraction
authors: Aharon Abadi, Ran Ettinger, Yishai A. Feldman
year: 2012
venue: Fundamental Approaches to Software Engineering (FASE 2012), Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol. 7212, Springer
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-3-642-28872-2_32
keywords: fine slicing, cut points, computation extraction, loop untangling, executable slice, co-slice
content:
  CORE CONCEPT: Fine slicing = program slicing where the programmer can SPECIFY WHICH
  DEPENDENCES to exclude ("cut points"), producing a more precisely bounded and executable slice.
  
  STANDARD SLICING PROBLEM: A backward slice of a statement in a loop often includes the
  entire loop (because loop iterations are entangled). Standard slices cannot "separate"
  computations intertwined in a loop.
  
  FINE SLICE with cut points:
    Given a slicing criterion C and a set of CUT POINTS P (dependences to exclude),
    fine_slice(C, P) = the maximal subset of program statements that:
    - Contains all statements that contribute to C via paths NOT cut by P
    - Is EXECUTABLE (semantically valid, produces correct values for C)
    - Can be EXTRACTED as an independent function
  
  CO-SLICE: The complementary slice to a fine slice. If fine_slice(C,P) is extracted,
  co-slice(C,P) is the remaining computation. Both the fine slice and co-slice are
  individually executable and correct.
  
  LOOP UNTANGLING: Fine slices can separate loop-interleaved computations:
    e.g., a loop that computes sum AND count simultaneously can be split into
    a fine slice for sum computation and a fine slice for count computation,
    even though they share the loop variable i.
  
  ALGORITHM STAGES:
    1. Core slice computation: follow unfiltered dependences backward from C
    2. Semantic restoration: add necessary branch tests to make slice executable
    3. Data-flow path compression: handle loop variable access patterns
    4. Control-flow path compression: add minimal control structure
  
  COMPUTATION EXTRACTION ALGORITHM:
    1. Input: program P, slicing criterion SC
    2. Compute fine slice FS
    3. Derive co-slice by disconnecting return values per SC
    4. Introduce containers for multi-value parameter passing across the boundary
    5. Insert method calls between extracted components
  
  PRACTICAL TOOL: Prototype implemented for COBOL and Java at IBM Research -- Haifa.
  
  SIGNIFICANCE FOR pflow: Fine slicing supports extraction of non-contiguous code and
  can untangle computations intertwined in loops -- the hardest case for standard slicing-based
  extraction approaches. The cut-point mechanism gives fine-grained control over what to
  include in an extraction.
  
  DOI: 10.1007/978-3-642-28872-2_32
<<<END>>>

