id: 22
ts: 2026-05-29T10:27:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: An Introduction to Slice-Based Cohesion and Coupling Metrics
authors: P. D. Green, P. C. R. Lane, A. Rainer, S. Scholz
year: 2009
venue: Technical Report, University of Hertfordshire, UK
quality: DOCS
url: https://uhra.herts.ac.uk/id/eprint/12083/
keywords: survey, slice-based metrics, output variables, step-by-step calculation, CodeSurfer
content:
  SCOPE: Survey paper synthesizing all slice-based cohesion and coupling metrics from
  Weiser 1984 through 2009. Addresses two gaps in existing literature:
  1. Output variables: surveys the variation in how different research groups define them
  2. Worked step-by-step calculation examples using CodeSurfer output
  
  OUTPUT VARIABLE DEFINITION (survey result showing four elements):
    1. Function RETURN VALUE
    2. MODIFIED GLOBAL VARIABLES (variables declared outside function, modified by it)
    3. MODIFIED REFERENCE PARAMETERS (pass-by-reference params that are written to)
    4. Variables PRINTED or written to external output (files, stdout, etc.)
  
  KEY INSIGHT: The choice of output variable definition significantly affects all slice-based
  metric values. Researchers have used inconsistent definitions, making metric comparisons
  between studies unreliable.
  
  TOOL USED: CodeSurfer (GrammaTech) for generating SDG/PDG, then manual metric computation
  from CodeSurfer's slice output.
  
  METRICS COVERED:
    - Weiser (1984): Tightness, Coverage, Overlap
    - Ott/Thuss (1989, 1993): Metric slices, MinCoverage, MaxCoverage, Parallelism
    - Bieman/Ott (1994): SFC, WFC, Adhesiveness
    - Harman et al. (1997): Slice-based coupling (Inflow, Outflow)
  
  Alternative URL: https://uhra.herts.ac.uk/id/eprint/12083/1/slice-based-cohesion-and-coupling-metrics-intro.pdf
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 23
ts: 2026-05-29T10:28:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Refactoring via Program Slicing and Sliding
authors: (Authors include Harman group, Oxford/UCL affiliation; this is doctoral work by Mathieu Verbaere)
year: 2007
venue: Proceedings of the 23rd IEEE International Conference on Software Maintenance (ICSM 2007), IEEE
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings-article/icsm/2007/04362672/12OmNz5s0Ov
keywords: program sliding, refactoring, sliding transformation, slice-based, computation reordering
content:
  PROGRAM SLIDING: A transformation complementary to slicing.
  
  SLICING: extracts a subset of statements (projection).
  SLIDING: REORDERS statements while preserving semantics -- moves statements earlier or later
  in the program to enable subsequent slicing/extraction.
  
  The combination of sliding (to move related statements together) + slicing (to extract them)
  enables refactoring of programs where related computations are scattered through the code.
  
  THESIS CONTEXT: Mathieu Verbaere's Oxford MSc thesis "Program Slicing for Refactoring" (2004),
  supervised by Oege de Moor. The ICSM 2007 paper is a summary.
  
  SIGNIFICANCE: Addresses the case where a computation intended for extraction is not currently
  contiguous -- sliding creates contiguity, then slicing extracts.
  
  RELATED: Program Sliding, a follow-up paper (SpringerLink): extends sliding formalization.
  URL: https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-31057-7_31
<<<END>>>

