id: 29
ts: 2026-05-29T10:34:00Z
relevance: MED
title: CodeScene X-Ray (Function-level Technical Debt Analysis)
authors: CodeScene AB (Adam Tornhill)
year: 2017-present (commercial SaaS)
venue: Commercial product
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://codescene.io/docs/guides/technical/xray.html
keywords: change coupling, cyclomatic complexity, hotspot, function-level, temporal coupling
content:
  X-RAY ANALYSES method/function level within hotspot files.
  
  METRICS COMPUTED:
    - Cyclomatic complexity per function (threshold: >10 concerning, >25 = "maintenance nightmare")
    - Change frequency per function (from git history)
    - Lines of code per function
    - Internal change coupling: % of commits where two functions change together
    - Code similarity (copy-paste detection between functions)
  
  COUPLING SIGNAL: Functions that frequently change together (high change coupling %) →
  suggests temporal dependency → candidate for co-location or merge/split.
  
  PROXIMITY: Distance between temporally coupled functions (counted as intermediate functions).
  Recommendation to co-locate related functions.
  
  NOTE: Not slice-based. Uses VCS history + cyclomatic complexity, NOT data-flow analysis.
  Does NOT compute SFC/WFC/tightness/overlap.
  Provides actionable refactoring signals but at a coarser granularity than slice metrics.
  
  LIMITATION: X-Ray limited to 200 recent revisions per hotspot. Language support varies.
  Languages with cyclomatic complexity: Java, C#, C/C++, Objective-C, Kotlin, others.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 30
ts: 2026-05-29T10:40:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Identifying 'Linchpin Vertices' That Cause Large Dependence Clusters
authors: David Binkley, Mark Harman (and co-authors)
year: 2009
venue: 9th IEEE International Working Conference on Source Code Analysis and Manipulation (SCAM 2009)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220703744_Identifying_Linchpin_Vertices_That_Cause_Large_Dependence_Clusters
keywords: linchpin vertex, dependence cluster, refactoring target, global variable, algorithm
content:
  LINCHPIN VERTEX DEFINITION:
  A linchpin vertex (linchpin variable) is a vertex v in the SDG such that removing v
  (or breaking its dependences) would significantly REDUCE the size of dependence clusters.
  Linchpins "hold together" the cluster.
  
  MOTIVATION: Large dependence clusters signal code that is hard to decompose.
  But knowing a cluster exists doesn't tell WHERE to intervene. Linchpins are the
  specific variables/statements to target for refactoring.
  
  TYPICAL LINCHPINS: Global variables are the primary linchpins -- they cause many
  functions to mutually depend on each other. Eliminating/localizing a global variable
  can break a large cluster into smaller independent pieces.
  
  ALGORITHM: Iterative removal approach: for each vertex v, estimate the cluster size
  reduction if v were removed. Select top candidates (linchpins) for refactoring.
  
  LATER WORK: "Efficient Identification of Linchpin Vertices" (UCL Discovery:1388948) --
  improved algorithm achieving "almost two orders of magnitude" speedup tested on 38 programs
  with ~500K lines of code.
  
  FOR pflow: Given a function with a large dependence cluster, identify which variables
  (especially global reads/writes) cause the cluster. Suggest: "Eliminate global dependency
  on X to enable splitting this function."
<<<END>>>

