id: 31
ts: 2026-05-29T10:41:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Dependence Anti-Patterns
authors: David Binkley, Nicolas Gold, Mark Harman, Zheng Li
year: 2008
venue: 4th International ERCIM Workshop on Software Evolution and Evolvability (EVOL 2008)
quality: WORKSHOP
url: http://www0.cs.ucl.ac.uk/staff/mharman/evol08.pdf
keywords: dependence anti-patterns, structural problems, global variables, coupling, complexity, maintenance
content:
  CONCEPT: Dependence anti-patterns are dependence structures that MAY indicate problems
  for software maintenance and evolution. Not always bad -- they are "warnings to investigate."
  
  ANTI-PATTERN TYPES (from search results):
    - Large dependence clusters (mutually-dependent statement sets)
    - High coupling via global variables (many functions share global state)
    - Fan-in / fan-out imbalances in dependence graph
    - Tangled computations (low cohesion visible as scattered slice membership)
  
  EMPIRICAL STUDY: Six real-world programs (open source + production).
  
  RELATIONSHIP TO CLUSTERS: Dependence anti-patterns are the structural root causes that
  manifest as large dependence clusters in the MSG/B-MSG visualization.
  
  ACTIONABILITY: Anti-patterns identify WHAT to fix; linchpin identification identifies WHERE.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 32
ts: 2026-05-29T10:42:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: A Survey of Program Slicing Techniques
authors: Frank Tip
year: 1995
venue: Journal of Programming Languages, vol. 3, no. 3, pp. 121-189, September 1995
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.franktip.org/pubs/jpl1995.pdf
keywords: survey, backward slice, forward slice, dynamic slice, decomposition slice, chopping, taxonomy
content:
  COMPREHENSIVE SURVEY covering all major slicing variants as of 1995.
  1,700+ citations. Still the canonical reference for slicing terminology.
  
  TAXONOMY:
    - STATIC backward slice: S(P, <p,V>) = statements affecting V at p, regardless of input
    - STATIC forward slice: statements affected by V at p, regardless of input
    - DYNAMIC slice: slice conditioned on a specific execution trace
    - DECOMPOSITION slice: D(P, v) = statements affecting v at ANY program point (Gallagher/Lyle 1991)
    
  CHOPPING: "determining the subset of the program's statements that cause influences of
  source elements to reach sink elements" -- defined as a generalization of slicing.
  
  KEY DEFINITIONS:
    - Slicing criterion: <p, V> where p = program point, V = variable set
    - Slice: subset of statements (or reduced program) preserving behavior w.r.t. criterion
    - Static slice: over-approximates dynamic behavior (may include statements not executed)
    
  DECOMPOSITION SLICING (Section on Gallagher/Lyle):
    D(P, v) = union of all S(P, <p, {v}>) for all program points p.
    Forms a lattice under set inclusion. Independent variables have disjoint slices.
  
  NOTE: PDF extraction failed (binary encoded); details from secondary sources.
  
  DOI / Citation: Frank Tip. "A survey of program slicing techniques." J. Programming Languages, 3(3):121-189, 1995.
<<<END>>>

