relevance: MED
title: An Empirical Investigation into the Effect of Slice Types on Slice-Based Cohesion Metrics
authors: (multiple authors)
year: 2016
venue: Information and Software Technology (Elsevier), DOI: 10.1016/j.infsof.2016.04.001
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0950584916300556
keywords: end-slice, metric-slice, cohesion metrics, empirical, open-source, PCA
content:
  RESEARCH QUESTION: Should end-slices (backward slice from end of function) or metric-slices
  (Ott/Thuss union of backward + forward slices) be used for computing slice-based cohesion?
  
  METRICS STUDIED: Coverage, MaxCoverage, MinCoverage, Tightness, Overlap (five statement-level
  end-slice-based cohesion metrics).
  
  DATASET: 40 widely used open-source software systems.
  
  KEY FINDING: Correlation analysis and PCA show that end-slice-based and metric-slice-based
  cohesion metrics "essentially measure the same software cohesion dimensions."
  → Using either type of slice gives equivalent cohesion signals.
  
  PRACTICAL IMPLICATION FOR pflow: Standard backward slice (simpler to implement) is
  sufficient; metric-slices (more complex) do not provide additional cohesion information.
  
  Published April 2016.
  DOI: 10.1016/j.infsof.2016.04.001
<<<END>>>

