id: 37
ts: 2026-05-29T10:50:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Assessing the Impact of Global Variables on Program Dependence and Dependence Clusters
authors: Dave Binkley, Mark Harman, Youssef Hassoun, Syed Islam, Zheng Li, Kiarash Mahdavi
year: 2010
venue: Journal of Systems and Software, vol. 83, no. 1, pp. 96-107, January 2010
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0164121209000843
keywords: global variables, dependence clusters, pollution, program dependence, empirical study
cites: Binkley/Harman 2005, Harman et al. 2009 TOPLAS
content:
  SCOPE: Empirical study of 21 programs (~50K LOC) measuring global variables' impact on
  dependence cluster sizes.
  
  KEY FINDINGS:
    - Over half of studied programs have a global variable with significant impact on program dependence
    - A quarter of programs have a global variable SOLELY responsible for a dependence cluster
    - Global variables are the dominant cause of large dependence clusters
  
  ALGORITHM: Transformation-based analysis:
    For each global variable g, simulate removing/localizing g (replacing with parameter).
    Measure reduction in: (1) slice sizes, (2) cluster sizes, (3) overall dependence quantity.
    Variables with large reduction = high-impact "polluting" globals.
  
  FOR pflow: When analyzing a function with large dependence clusters:
    1. Identify global variable reads/writes within the function
    2. For each global g, measure how much cluster size decreases if g were localized
    3. Report: "Variable X is causing entanglement -- localizing it would enable splitting"
  
  DOI: 10.1016/j.jss.2009.08.045
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 38
ts: 2026-05-29T10:51:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Cognitive Complexity -- A New Way of Measuring Understandability (White Paper)
authors: G. Ann Campbell (SonarSource)
year: 2018 (v1.0), updated 2021
venue: SonarSource White Paper / ACM TechDebt 2018 (conference version)
quality: INDUSTRY
url: https://www.sonarsource.com/resources/cognitive-complexity/
keywords: cognitive complexity, cyclomatic complexity alternative, nesting, control flow, understandability
content:
  COGNITIVE COMPLEXITY DEFINITION:
  A metric for measuring how hard a function is to understand, not just how many paths.
  
  INCREMENT RULES:
    +1 for each: if, else if, else, switch, for, while, do-while, catch, ternary operator,
    boolean sequence of operators (each sequence of && or || counts once)
    
    ADDITIONAL NESTING PENALTY: +1 for each level of nesting at the time of the increment.
    Deeply nested ifs cost more than shallow ones.
    
    NO increment for: logical operators in simple expressions, early returns
  
  DIFFERENCE FROM CYCLOMATIC:
    - Cyclomatic: counts linearly independent paths (each boolean operator = +1)
    - Cognitive: penalizes nesting; doesn't count boolean operators individually;
      counts sequences of same operator as one increment
    
  REFACTORING SIGNAL: High cognitive complexity → deeply nested, hard to extract.
  Functions with CC > 15-25 are typically flagged for decomposition.
  
  ACM paper: "Cognitive complexity" at 2018 International Conference on Technical Debt.
  DOI: 10.1145/3194164.3194186
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 39
ts: 2026-05-29T10:52:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Using Maximal Slices as a Solution to the 'Output Variable' Problem for Calculating Slice-Based Cohesion Metrics
authors: James Hamilton
year: (date unknown, published on jameshamilton.eu research page)
venue: Research paper / technical report (Dr James Hamilton)
quality: BLOG
url: https://jameshamilton.eu/research/using-maximal-slices-solution-output-variable-problem-calculating-slice-based-cohesion
keywords: maximal slice, output variable problem, cohesion metrics, slice-based, normalization
content:
  THE OUTPUT VARIABLE PROBLEM:
  Different researchers define "output variables" differently, leading to:
    - Different sets of slicing criteria per function
    - Incomparable metric values across studies
    
  MAXIMAL SLICES SOLUTION:
  Define cohesion metrics using MAXIMAL SLICES instead of output-variable slices.
  A maximal slice MS(P) is a slice that is not a proper subset of any other slice of P.
    MS is maximal iff there is no other slice S' such that MS ⊂ S'.
  
  KEY PROPERTY: A maximal slice cannot be a "sub-task" of a larger task within the function.
  It represents the largest consistent dependency closure for its criterion.
  
  BENEFIT: Eliminates the need to define "output variables" -- computation is based on
  the structure of the program alone.
  
  COHESION REDEFINED: Compute Tightness/Coverage/etc using only maximal slices as criteria.
  Functions with many non-overlapping maximal slices have low cohesion (multiple independent
  maximal computations).
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 40
ts: 2026-05-29T10:55:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: A Vocabulary of Program Slicing-Based Techniques
authors: Josep Silva
year: 2012
venue: ACM Computing Surveys, vol. 44, no. 3, article 12, June 2012
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2187671.2187674
keywords: taxonomy, decomposition slicing, chopping, backward slice, forward slice, survey, classification
content:
  COMPREHENSIVE TAXONOMY of 30+ slicing-based techniques.
  
  VERBATIM DEFINITION OF DECOMPOSITION SLICING (Section 2.25, citing Gallagher/Lyle 1991):
    "Decomposition slicing was introduced in the context of software maintenance to capture
    all computation on a given variable. The objective of this technique is to extract those
    program statements which are needed to compute the values of a given variable."
    
    CRITERION: Single variable v (no program point required).
    
    COMPUTATION:
    "The slice is built from the union of the static backward slices constructed for the
    criteria {<n1, v>, ..., <nm, v>, <end, v>} where {n1,...,nm} is the set of lines in
    which v is output and end is the 'End' of the program."
    
    INSTANCE: "decomposition slicing is an instance of simultaneous slicing where the set
    of slicing criteria is derived from the variable and the program."
    
    QUESTION ANSWERED: "What statements of the program can affect this variable?"
    MAIN APPLICATIONS: Software maintenance, testing, program comprehension.
  
  VERBATIM DEFINITION OF CHOPPING (Section 2.6, citing Jackson/Rollins 1994):
    "In chopping, the slicing criterion selects two sets of variables, source and sink, and
    then it computes all the statements in the program that being affected by source, they
    affect sink. Therefore, chopping is a generalization of both forward and backward slicing
    where either source or sink is empty."
    
    "As noted by Reps and Rosay [1995], chopping is particularly useful to detect those
    statements that 'transmit effects' from one part of the program (source) to another (sink)."
    
    QUESTION ANSWERED: "What program statements can influence these variables at this statement
    while they are influenced by these (other) variables at this other statement?"
    MAIN APPLICATIONS: Program analysis and debugging.
  
  EXAMPLE CHOP (from paper):
    Program: augmented word-count with variables lines, chars, subtext.
    chop(source={line 3, vars={lines}}, sink={line 16, vars={lines}}) =
      {lines=1, while loop body with lines update, write(lines)}
    
    chop(source={line 3, vars={lines}}, sink={line 9, vars={lines}}) =
      {lines=1; lines=lines+1;}
    [Chop is much smaller than full backward/forward slices!]
  
  RELATIONSHIPS BETWEEN TECHNIQUES (from Figure 29):
    Decomposition slice IS-A Simultaneous slice (special case with criteria derived from program)
    Chopping IS-A Generalization of both forward and backward slicing
    Chopping = intersection of forward_slice(source) and backward_slice(sink) [intraprocedural]
    Context-sensitive chopping (Reps/Rosay) is strictly more precise than naive intersection
  
  DOI: 10.1145/2187671.2187674
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 41
ts: 2026-05-29T11:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Interprocedural Slicing Using Dependence Graphs (SDG Paper -- Additional Technical Details)
authors: Susan Horwitz, Thomas Reps, David Binkley
year: 1990
venue: ACM TOPLAS vol. 12, no. 1, January 1990 (journal version of PLDI 1988)
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.csa.iisc.ac.in/~raghavan/CleanedPav2011/horwitz-sdg-slicing-1990.pdf
keywords: SDG construction, summary edges, attribute grammar, linkage grammar, two-pass algorithm
content:
  SDG CONSTRUCTION DETAILS (extracted from verbatim PDF text):
  
  "A system dependence graph includes a program dependence graph, which represents the
  system's main program, procedure dependence graphs, which represent the system's auxiliary
  procedures, and some additional edges. These additional edges are of two sorts:
  (1) edges that represent direct dependences between a call site and the called procedure,
  (2) edges that represent transitive dependences due to calls."
  
  PARAMETER PASSING NODES (to handle value-result parameters at call sites):
    - ACTUAL-IN node: represents actual parameter being passed in (at call site)
    - ACTUAL-OUT node: represents actual parameter being passed back out (at return)
    - FORMAL-IN node: formal parameter received at procedure entry
    - FORMAL-OUT node: formal parameter being returned
    
    PARAMETER-IN EDGE: actual_in →_pi formal_in
    PARAMETER-OUT EDGE: formal_out →_po actual_out
    CALL EDGE: call_site →_call procedure_entry
    
  SUMMARY EDGES: Key innovation.
    A SUMMARY EDGE actual_in →_sum actual_out means:
    "The value of actual_in at the call site transitively affects actual_out after the return."
    Computed using the LINKAGE GRAMMAR (attribute grammar representing call/param relationships).
    Summary edges allow interprocedural slices to be computed in two graph traversal passes.
  
  TWO-PASS SLICING ALGORITHM:
    Pass 1: Backward traversal from target, crossing only:
      - Intra-procedural edges (control, flow, def-order within a procedure)
      - PARAMETER-OUT edges (formal_out → actual_out: going from callee to caller)
      - SUMMARY edges (short-circuit of call/return through a call site)
      Do NOT cross CALL edges (actual → formal: going INTO callee) in Pass 1.
    
    Pass 2: Another backward traversal, crossing only:
      - Intra-procedural edges
      - CALL edges (into callees)
      - PARAMETER-IN edges (actual_in → formal_in)
      Do NOT cross PARAMETER-OUT or SUMMARY edges in Pass 2.
    
    Combined result: precise context-sensitive interprocedural slice.
    
  CORRECTNESS: Two-pass prevents the calling-context problem that affected Weiser's algorithm.
  "Weiser's method... fails to account for the calling context of a called procedure."
  
  COMPLEXITY NOTES: Building SDG (including summary edges): expensive (polynomial in edge count).
  Slicing given SDG: O(V+E) per slice.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 42
ts: 2026-05-29T11:01:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Precise Interprocedural Chopping (Additional Complexity Details)
authors: Thomas Reps, Genevieve Rosay
year: 1995
venue: Proc. 3rd ACM SIGSOFT Symposium on Foundations of Software Engineering (FSE-3), Washington DC, pp. 41-49
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/222132.222138
keywords: interprocedural chopping, context-sensitive, SDG, summary edges, complexity
content:
  COMPLEXITY:
    Assumes SDG's summary edges already computed in:
    O((P × E × Params) + (CSites × Params³))
    where P = procedures, E = edges, Params = parameters per procedure, CSites = call sites.
    
    The chopping algorithm itself: not simple linear time.
    "The algorithm is, however, rather complicated to implement."
  
  CONTEXT-SENSITIVITY: First context-sensitive interprocedural chopping algorithm.
  Distinguishes different invocations of the same procedure (different call sites get different slices).
  
  KEY DIFFERENCE from Jackson/Rollins 1994:
    - Jackson/Rollins: intraprocedural only (source and target in same procedure)
    - Reps/Rosay: full interprocedural with correct calling context
  
  DOI: 10.1145/222132.222138
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 43
ts: 2026-05-29T11:05:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Extract Method Refactoring by Successive Edge Contraction
authors: Omkarendra Tiwari, Rushikesh K. Joshi
year: 2019
venue: arXiv:1908.04636 [cs.SE], August 2019
quality: PREPRINT
url: https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.04636
keywords: structure dependence graph, edge contraction, segmentation, extract method, language independent
content:
  CORE APPROACH: "Segmentation" -- identify method sub-tasks by contracting edges in a
  Structure Dependence Graph (SDG).
  
  NOTE: This paper's SDG is different from Horwitz/Reps/Binkley's SDG (System Dependence Graph).
  Here SDG = Structure Dependence Graph = a method-level dependence representation.
  
  STRUCTURE DEPENDENCE GRAPH:
    Nodes = statements in a method
    Edges = data and control dependence relationships (similar to PDG but method-local)
    Then collapsed into "segment graph" capturing dependence between sub-tasks.
  
  THREE EDGE CONTRACTIONS (determine how to identify segments):
    1. STRUCTURAL CONTROL EDGE CONTRACTION: Contract control dependence edges
       (merge control-dependent statements with their controlling predicate).
    2. EXCLUSIVE DATA DEPENDENCE EDGE CONTRACTION: Contract edges where data flows
       exclusively within a sub-computation (no data crossing the potential boundary).
    3. SEQUENTIAL DATA DEPENDENCE EDGE CONTRACTION: Contract edges for sequential
       data flow (def-use chains within a linear sequence).
  
  SEGMENT GRAPH: After contractions, remaining graph nodes = "segments" = extractable chunks.
  Non-adjacent segments with thin interfaces between them are extraction candidates.
  
  LANGUAGE INDEPENDENCE: The intermediate SDG representation is language-agnostic.
  
  EVALUATION: Four case studies (three open source).
  
  KEY INSIGHT FOR pflow: Successive edge contraction = a different way to find extraction
  boundaries than slice-based approaches. Start with all dependencies, then collapse
  "tightly coupled" pairs iteratively. What's left = natural partition of the function.
  
  COMPARISON TO SLICING APPROACH: Instead of "compute slice, check if extractable",
  this approach "contract strongly-coupled statements into atomic segments, then find
  optimal segment groupings."
<<<END>>>

