  LIBRARY: python_graphs (open source, Apache/CC-BY 4.0)
  LANGUAGE: Python
  
  GRAPH TYPES:
    - Control-flow graphs (CFG)
    - Data-flow graphs (DFG)
    - Composite "program graphs" (combine CFG, DFG, syntactic, lexical)
  
  USE CASE: Primarily for ML over code (training datasets for neural models), not directly
  for cohesion metrics. But provides the underlying graph structures needed.
  
  NOTE: Does not implement slice-based cohesion metrics (SFC/WFC/tightness) out of the box.
  Provides the infrastructure (CFG, data-flow graphs) that would be needed as input.
  
  AVAILABLE: open source on GitHub (Google Research).
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 45
ts: 2026-05-29T11:10:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Using a Concept Lattice of Decomposition Slices for Program Understanding and Impact Analysis
authors: Paolo Tonella
year: 2003
venue: IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering, vol. 29, no. 6, pp. 495-509, June 2003
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1109/TSE.2003.1205178
keywords: concept lattice, decomposition slices, slice graph, program understanding, impact analysis, formal concept analysis
cites: Gallagher/Lyle 1991
content:
  EXTENSION OF GALLAGHER/LYLE 1991: Applies formal concept analysis to decomposition slices
  to create a richer program representation.
  
  DECOMPOSITION SLICE GRAPH (recap from Gallagher/Lyle 1991):
    Nodes = decomposition slices D(P, vi) for each output variable vi
    Edges = when D(P, vi) ∩ D(P, vj) ≠ ∅ (non-empty intersection = dependence)
    Partitions program into computations on different variables.
    Shows dependence relations between computations.
  
  CONCEPT LATTICE OF DECOMPOSITION SLICES (Tonella's contribution):
    Uses formal concept analysis to organize decomposition slices into a hierarchy:
    - Nodes = "concepts" where a concept = (extent, intent):
        extent = set of output variables sharing a common slice subset
        intent = the common slice subset
    - Edges = generalization/specialization relationships between concepts
    
    The lattice is an EXTENSION of the decomposition slice graph:
    "The concept lattice of decomposition slices... is obtained through concept analysis,
    with additional nodes associated with weak interferences between computations."
    
    BENEFIT OVER SLICE GRAPH: Lattice reveals PARTIAL OVERLAP patterns that the simple
    slice graph (which only shows binary edge/no-edge) cannot capture.
  
  APPLICATION: Impact analysis -- given a change to variable v, the lattice identifies
  which other variables are affected and to what degree.
  
  DOI: 10.1109/TSE.2003.1205178
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 46
ts: 2026-05-29T11:11:00Z
relevance: MED
title: Are Decomposition Slices Clones?
authors: Keith B. Gallagher
year: 2003
venue: 3rd IEEE International Workshop on Source Code Analysis and Manipulation (SCAM 2003)
quality: WORKSHOP
url: http://www.cs.loyola.edu/~kbg/clone.pdf
keywords: decomposition slices, clone detection, slice graph, program comprehension
content:
  QUESTION: When computing all decomposition slices for a program, many slices are identical.
  Are identical decomposition slices a form of code clone?
  
  DECOMPOSITION SLICE GRAPH (confirmed definition):
    Partitions program into computations performed on different variables.
    Shows dependence relations: when computation A needs computation B as a building block,
    there is an edge from A to B in the graph.
  
  FINDING: Arguments BOTH for and against treating identical decomposition slices as clones:
    - FOR: Some identical slices arise from "failure to note appropriate abstractions"
      (same pattern used repeatedly without extracting to a function)
    - AGAINST: Slice-clones do not arise from programmer intent or copying
    
  PRACTICAL IMPLICATION: Identical decomposition slices = candidates for "Extract Function"
  (common computation that appears multiple times = should be a shared helper function).
<<<END>>>


