id: 47
ts: 2026-05-29T11:15:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Bieman/Ott 1994 Worked Example (canonical numbers from paper)
authors: James M. Bieman, Linda M. Ott
year: 1994
venue: IEEE TSE vol. 20 no. 8 (same as Record id:2), extracted from paper's Figure 4 example
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://www.cs.colostate.edu/~bieman/Pubs/tse94new.pdf
keywords: worked example, SFC, WFC, adhesiveness, data tokens, glue tokens, calculation
content:
  CANONICAL WORKED EXAMPLE from paper Figure 4 (procedure SA):
    Total data tokens in procedure: 11
    Glue tokens: 5 (appear in ≥2 data slices)
    Superglue tokens: 2 (appear in all data slices)
    
    BREAKDOWN of glue token distribution:
      - 2 tokens appear in 3 slices (superglue OR near-superglue, contribution to adhesiveness: 2×3)
      - 3 tokens appear in 2 slices (plain glue, contribution: 3×2)
    
    CALCULATIONS:
      WFC(SA) = 5/11 = 0.455
      SFC(SA) = 2/11 = 0.182
      A(SA) = (2×3 + 3×2) / (11 × number_of_slices)
             = (6 + 6) / denominator = 0.36
      
      [NOTE: exact denominator depends on k=number of slices; if k=3 then denominator=33,
       so A = 12/33 = 0.364 ≈ 0.36. Confirms k=3 slices (3 output variables) in this example.]
  
  INTERPRETATION OF THESE VALUES:
    WFC=0.455: About half of data tokens are shared across ≥2 computations (moderate cohesion)
    SFC=0.182: Only 2/11 tokens appear in ALL slices (weak universal core)
    A=0.36: Low-to-moderate adhesiveness overall
    
    REFACTORING SIGNAL from these values: Function has some shared computation (WFC>0) but
    no strong unified core (low SFC). Multiple partially-overlapping computations.
    Candidate for careful decomposition examining which pairs of output variables have
    highest pairwise cohesion (Jaccard similarity of their slices).
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 48
ts: 2026-05-29T11:16:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CONSOLIDATED FORMULAS REFERENCE -- Slice-Based Cohesion Metrics
authors: [synthesized from Weiser 1984, Ott/Thuss 1989/1993, Bieman/Ott 1994]
year: N/A
venue: N/A (synthesis record)
quality: DOCS
url: N/A
keywords: tightness, coverage, overlap, SFC, WFC, adhesiveness, min-coverage, max-coverage, parallelism, formulas
content:
  NOTATION:
    M = module (function) being measured
    |M| = number of statements in M
    k = number of output variables (= number of slices/data-slices)
    Si = backward slice (or metric slice) for output variable i, |Si| = slice size
    S_all = S1 ∩ S2 ∩ ... ∩ Sk (intersection of ALL slices)
    DT = set of all data tokens in M, |DT| = total data token count
    GT = set of glue tokens (in ≥2 data slices)
    SGT = set of superglue tokens (in all k data slices)
    A(t) = adhesiveness of token t = number of data slices containing t
  
  WEISER (1984) STATEMENT-LEVEL METRICS (use full slices, not data-token-level):
  
    TIGHTNESS:       T(M)    = |S_all| / |M|
    COVERAGE:        Cov(M)  = (1/k) × sum_i(|Si|) / |M|
    OVERLAP:         OL(M)   = (1/k) × sum_i(|Si ∩ S_all| / |Si|)
  
  OTT/THUSS (1989, 1993) ADDITIONAL METRICS (use metric slices = backward ∪ forward):
  
    MIN-COVERAGE:    MinCov(M) = min_i(|Si|) / |M|
    MAX-COVERAGE:    MaxCov(M) = max_i(|Si|) / |M|
    PARALLELISM:     PAR(M)   = 1 - (1/k) × sum_i(|Si ∩ S_all| / |Si|)  [= 1 - OL]
  
  BIEMAN/OTT (1994) DATA-TOKEN-LEVEL METRICS:
  
    WFC(M) = |GT| / |DT|
           = count(tokens in ≥2 data slices) / count(all data tokens)
    
    SFC(M) = |SGT| / |DT|
           = count(tokens in ALL k data slices) / count(all data tokens)
    
    A(M) = sum_{t ∈ GT} A(t) / (|DT| × k)
         = (sum of per-glue-token slice-membership counts) / (total tokens × slice count)
  
  USEFUL DERIVED SIGNALS FOR pflow:
  
    SPLIT SIGNAL: SFC = 0 AND WFC < threshold → function has no universal core and
      little sharing → multiple independent computations → strong split candidate.
    
    TIGHTNESS SIGNAL: T near 0 → no statement common to all slices → no unified core.
    
    HIGH PARALLELISM: PAR near 1 → slices barely overlap → fully parallel computations
      → clean split possible.
    
    PAIRWISE COHESION (for choosing WHICH variables to group when splitting):
      C(vi, vj) = |S_i ∩ S_j| / |S_i ∪ S_j|  (Jaccard similarity)
      Group variables with high C(vi,vj); extract each group as separate function.
      
    THINNEST SEAM: For each candidate split point p in a function F (where statements
      before p and after p form two groups G1, G2):
      SEAM_WIDTH(p) = count of variables that are both DEFINED in G1 and USED in G2
                    + count of variables DEFINED in G2 and USED in G1
      Choose p that minimizes SEAM_WIDTH(p).
      Alternatively: for decomposition slices D(F,{outputs_G1}) and D(F,{outputs_G2}):
      INTERFACE_SIZE = |D(F,{o_G1}) ∩ D(F,{o_G2})| 
      (size of shared statements = coupling at the boundary).
      Minimize INTERFACE_SIZE to find the thinnest seam.
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 49
ts: 2026-05-29T11:17:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: ALGORITHM SKETCH -- Slice-Based Refactoring / Function Decomposition for pflow
authors: [synthesized from Gallagher/Lyle 1991, Bieman/Ott 1994, Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou 2011, Gold/Harman 1999]
year: N/A
venue: N/A (synthesis record)
quality: DOCS
url: N/A
keywords: algorithm, pflow, function splitting, slice-based, cohesion, seam finding, refactoring
content:
  ALGORITHM FOR SLICE-BASED FUNCTION DECOMPOSITION IN pflow:
  
  INPUT: Function F with CFG, dominators/post-dominators, def-use chains (already built by pflow).
         Output variables of F: {o1, o2, ..., ok} (return val, global writes, ref-param writes, output calls)
  
  STEP 1 -- COMPUTE DECOMPOSITION SLICES:
    For each output variable oi:
      D(F, oi) = union of backward_slice(F, <p, oi>) for all p where oi is used/defined.
      (In practice: build PDG from pflow's CFG + def-use, then do backward reachability from
       all final def/use nodes of oi.)
  
  STEP 2 -- COMPUTE COHESION METRICS:
    S_all = D(F, o1) ∩ D(F, o2) ∩ ... ∩ D(F, ok)
    T(F) = |S_all| / |F|                              (tightness)
    Cov(F) = (1/k) × sum_i |D(F,oi)| / |F|           (coverage)
    
    For Bieman/Ott metrics: enumerate data tokens in each data slice.
    WFC(F), SFC(F), A(F) as per formulas in Record id:48.
  
  STEP 3 -- DETECT DEPENDENCE CLUSTERS:
    Compute backward_slice(s) for each statement s in F.
    Find plateau in MSG: statements with same slice size → dependence cluster.
    If large cluster exists (>50% of F): report "entangled core, consider fixing globals first."
    Identify linchpin variables (global reads/writes) causing the cluster.
  
  STEP 4 -- FIND SPLIT CANDIDATES (if T(F) < threshold or SFC(F) ≈ 0):
  
    4a. PAIRWISE COHESION APPROACH (Gold/Harman 1999):
        For each pair (oi, oj): C(oi,oj) = |D(F,oi) ∩ D(F,oj)| / |D(F,oi) ∪ D(F,oj)|
        Cluster output variables by C values (low C = unrelated → different extracted functions).
        
    4b. DISJOINT SLICE APPROACH (Gallagher/Lyle 1991):
        Group output variables into G1, G2, ... such that slices for each group are
        nearly/fully disjoint from slices for other groups.
        If D(F, G1) ∩ D(F, G2) = ∅: clean split possible.
        If D(F, G1) ∩ D(F, G2) ≠ ∅: shared statements must be either:
          (a) duplicated (if safe), or
          (b) extracted into a helper function called by both halves.
  
  STEP 5 -- VERIFY EXTRACTABILITY (Tsantalis/Chatzigeorgiou 2011 rules):
    For each proposed extraction E = D(F, Gi):
    - E forms a valid SESE region (single-entry single-exit in CFG)?
      If not: extend to nearest SESE boundary (the "Wedge" step from Lakhotia/Deprez 1998).
    - Extraction would not require duplicating object-state mutations?
    - Extraction would not violate anti-dependences or output-dependences?
  
  STEP 6 -- COMPUTE BOUNDARY (what crosses the boundary):
    INTERFACE(F, Gi, Gj) = {variables v | v is defined in D(F,Gi) and used in D(F,Gj)}
                           ∪ {variables v | v is defined in D(F,Gj) and used in D(F,Gi)}
    Report: "Splitting at this boundary requires passing N variables across the boundary: {list}."
    Lower INTERFACE size → thinner seam → easier/cleaner split.
  
  STEP 7 -- CHOPPING FOR EXPLANATION (Reps/Rosay 1995):
    For each significant data flow in F (source_stmt → sink_stmt):
    chop(source_stmt, sink_stmt) = path of statements transmitting effects.
    Report: "These statements implement the computation from X to Y."
    Useful for explaining WHAT each slice does (not just WHERE to split).
  
  OUTPUT (what pflow should report):
    - Cohesion metrics: T, Cov, SFC, WFC, A
    - Dependence cluster presence and size (with linchpin identification)
    - Split recommendations: "Split into F1({o1}) and F2({o2,o3}), boundary passes {v1, v2}"
    - SESE region boundaries for each proposed extraction
    - Tightness of proposed extracted functions (will extraction improve cohesion?)
<<<END>>>

